1:20@0-100=325, MR1:30@8-100=216.7.\n\nGreat, that's 19 rows consistent with Table 2.\n\nNote Qmin \"-\" means not applicable for that (MR, pressure range) combination.\n\nTable 5 — Verification scale interval by Type (G-value):\n- G65: drums before comma 6, behind comma 2, control element 0.002 m³\n- G100–G1600: 7, 1, 0.02 m³\n- G2000–G4000: 8, 0, 0.2 m³\n\n**Model family:** 19 models. model_id: I'll use \"G65\", \"G100-DN80\", ... Hmm, duplicates: G160 (DN80, DN100), G250 (DN80, DN100), G400 (DN100, DN150), G650 (DN150, DN200), G1000 (DN150, DN200, DN250), G1600 (DN200, DN250, DN300), G2500 (DN250, DN300). So need DN in model_id. Format: \"DN80-G100\" or \"G100/DN80\". I'll use \"DN80-G100\" style. Actually maybe clearer: \"G100 (DN80)\". I'll go with \"DN80-G100\" — concise and sortable. Hmm, but Table 5 uses Type = G-value alone. The actual meter type designation is TBQM + presumably G-value. The certificate type is TBQM. The \"Type\" column in Table 5 shows \"G65\", \"G100 – G1600\", \"G2000 – G4000\" — this suggests type designations TBQM G65, etc. I'll keep model ids with DN+G and record type_designations in certified_type as [\"TBQM\"].\n\n**model_level characteristics:** For each of the 19 models: diameter (mm), g_value, impeller_diameter, blade_height, vane_thickness, blade_angle, number_of_blades, qmax, qt, bearing characteristics (main shaft inlet/outlet, Cr, C0r), verification scale interval info (drums before/behind comma, control element value).\n\nThat's a lot. Let me decide what to include in model_level vs matrix_tables. The schema says: matrix_tables for multi-dim tables, AND populate model_family.models + characteristics.model_level (per the R137 example in the prompt! This is literally an R137 example). The example shows matrix_tables with columns and rows, plus model_level for qmax/qt/qmin.\n\nGiven the complexity here (multiple tables keyed by DN+G), I'll:\n- model_family.models: 19 entries with model_id like \"DN50-G65\", and attributes maybe minimal (or put diameter/g in attributes? The schema shows models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put attributes: {diameter_mm: 80, g_value: 100}. That's helpful. But then also model_level entries. Hmm, duplication. I'll put core identifying attributes in models[].attributes (diameter, g_value) and detailed varying characteristics in model_level.\n- matrix_tables: I'll emit Table 2, Table 3, Table 4, Table 5 as matrix tables with proper rows (expanding rowspans). \n- model_level: include key attributes: diameter_mm, g_value, qmax (m³/h), qt (m³/h), verification_scale_interval/control_element maybe.\n\nFor Qmin — it varies by configuration (MR and pressure range), so it's config_level. Conditions: \"MR 1:20, pressure range 0–100 bar(g)\" etc. I could emit config_level entries with attribute \"minimum_flow_rate_qmin\", axis \"measuring_ratio_and_pressure_range\", values: {condition: \"MR 1:20, 0-100 bar(g)\", model: ..., value: ...}. Hmm, config_level schema: {attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}}. But it also varies by model. The schema doesn't nest model inside config_level. Hmm. Maybe put qmin per model+config into matrix_tables rows (already done in Table 4), and skip config_level to avoid messy hybrid. Or emit config_level with conditions that embed model: too ugly.\n\nAlternative: model_level entries for qmin_mr1_20_0_100_bar, qmin_mr1_20_8_100_bar, qmin_mr1_30_8_100_bar, qmin_mr1_30_16_100_bar — each attribute with 19 values, using null for \"-\". That fits the model×attribute matrix nicely! Attribute names encode the config. Value null for not-applicable. That's clean and machine-usable. I'll do that.\n\nAlso bearing characteristics per DN (not per G): main_shaft_inlet, main_shaft_outlet, dynamic_load_rating_cr_inlet, dynamic_load_rating_cr_outlet, static_load_rating_c0r_inlet, static_load_rating_c0r_outlet — these vary by DN only; since models are DN+G, they're model_level (constant within DN). I'll include them in model_level with 19 values each (repeated per model). That's fine. Or keep them only in matrix_tables to reduce bloat. Hmm. The instruction for R137 example: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So model_level should get the important ones. To balance size, I'll include in model_level: diameter_mm, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin×4, impeller_diameter, blade_height, vane_thickness, number_of_blades, blade_angle? Blade angle is 45 for all — constant → type_level: blade_angle 45 degrees. Constant ones go to type_level.\n\nConstants across all models: blade_angle = 45°. impeller/blade/vane/blades vary by DN. Bearing stuff varies by DN. Verification interval varies by G-range.\n\nType_level (Table 1): as listed above.\n\nLet me also handle \"Maximum pressure\": type_level string \"See tables 2 and 4\". And from Table 3: max operating pressure 16 bar(g) for permanently lubricated (DN50-100), 100 bar(g) for external oil pump lubricated. I could add config_level: attribute \"maximum_operating_pressure\", axis \"bearing_lubrication_type\", values: [{condition: \"permanently lubricated bearings (DN 50-100)\", value: 16, unit \"bar(g)\"}, {condition: \"external oil pump lubricated bearings (DN 150-300)\", value: 100, unit \"bar(g)\"}]. Good use of config_level.\n\nInlet pipe length: 2 DN — type_level: inlet_pipe_length: {value: 2, unit: \"DN\"}. That's from installation conditions. I'll add \"prescribed_inlet_pipe_length\" = 2 DN for severe flow disturbances... The text: \"must be avoided within the prescribed inlet pipe length, which is 2 DN\" for severe disturbances; \"For mild flow disturbances, there is no prescribed inlet pipe length.\" I'll add type_level: prescribed_inlet_pipe_length_severe_disturbances: {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"DN\"}. Maybe config_level axis \"flow_disturbance\": severe → 2 DN; mild → none. That's nice config_level. I'll do config_level: attribute \"prescribed_inlet_pipe_length\", axis \"flow_disturbance_severity\", values: [{condition: \"severe flow disturbances\", value: 2, unit \"DN\"}, {condition: \"mild flow disturbances\", value: null}].\n\nInstallation positions: horizontal, vertical up, vertical down → covered by orientation in Table 1.\n\n**Footnotes:** none present. Empty footnotes[].\n\n**matrix_tables:**\n\nTable 2 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments – Measuring part\":\ncolumns: [\"diameter\", \"g_value\", \"impeller_diameter\", \"blade_height\", \"vane_thickness\", \"blade_angle\", \"number_of_blades\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"°\", null]\nrows: 19 rows with numbers. Note vane thickness \"15,5\" → 15.5, \"35,5\" → 35.5.\n\nTable 3 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments – Bearings\":\ncolumns: [\"diameter\", \"main_shaft_inlet\", \"main_shaft_outlet\", \"dynamic_load_rating_inlet\", \"dynamic_load_rating_outlet\", \"static_load_rating_inlet\", \"static_load_rating_outlet\", \"max_operating_pressure_16_bar_version\", \"max_operating_pressure_100_bar_version\"]\nHmm, the last two columns: header \"Maximum operating pressure\" with sub-columns \"16 bar(g)\" and \"100 bar(g)\". Cell content is the bearing type applicable at that pressure. So columns could be \"bearing_type_at_16_bar_g\" and \"bearing_type_at_100_bar_g\". Units: null, mm, mm, N, N, N, N, null, null.\nrows: 7 rows:\n- 50: 2,2,286,286,90,90,\"permanently lubricated bearings, double shielded\",\"external oil pump lubricated bearings, single or double shielded\"\n- 80: 3,3,644,644,215,215,same,same\n- 100: 4,4,1339,644,488,215,same,same\n- 150: 5,5,1646,1339,663,488,null (i.e. \"-\"),same\n- 200: 6,6,2522,2522,1057,1057,null,same\n- 250: 8,8,3369,3369,1363,1363,null,same\n- 300: 10,10,6100,6100,2600,2600,null,same\n\nTable 4 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments – Essential characteristics\":\ncolumns: [\"diameter\",\"g_value\",\"qmax\",\"qt\",\"qmin_mr_1_20_pressure_0_100_bar_g\",\"qmin_mr_1_20_pressure_8_100_bar_g\",\"qmin_mr_1_30_pressure_8_100_bar_g\",\"qmin_mr_1_30_pressure_16_100_bar_g\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"]\n19 rows. \"-\" → null. Decimals: 12,5→12.5; 3,3→3.3; 5,3→5.3; 8,3→8.3; 13,3→13.3; 21,7→21.7; 33,3→33.3; 53,3→53.3; 83,3→83.3; 133,3→133.3; 216,7→216.7.\n\nTable 5 \"Verification scale interval\":\ncolumns: [\"type\",\"drums_before_comma\",\"drums_behind_comma\",\"control_element\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, null, \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {type: \"G65\", drums_before_comma: 6, drums_behind_comma: 2, control_element: 0.002}\n- {type: \"G100 – G1600\", ...: 7, 1, 0.02}\n- {type: \"G2000 – G4000\", 8, 0, 0.2}\n\nfootnotes: none. Remarks regarding table 4 — I can put in description or footnotes of matrix_tables[2]. The remark about permanently lubricated limiting pressure to 16 bar → captured in config_level. MR definition: \"MR = measuring range (Qmax/Qmin = 1:20 or 1:30)\" → footnote-like. I'll add to Table 4's footnotes: e.g. {\"marker\": \"MR\", \"text\": \"MR = measuring range (Qmax / Qmin = 1:20 or 1:30)\"} — hmm, footnotes format is {marker, text}. The remark isn't a numbered footnote. I'll put it in \"description\" of the table instead. And add description mentioning \"-\" = not applicable.\n\n**model_family.models:** 19 entries:\nmodel_id: \"DN50-G65\" etc. attributes: {diameter_mm: X, g_value: Y}. label maybe \"TBQM G65 (DN 50)\".\n\n**model_level characteristics list:**\n\n1. diameter: unit \"mm\", 19 values.\n2. g_value: unit null, 19 values.\n3. impeller_diameter: \"mm\", per model (DN-constant).\n4. blade_height: \"mm\".\n5. vane_thickness: \"mm\" (15.5 for DN50, 22 DN80, 28 DN100, 27 DN150, 27 DN200, 30 DN250, 30 DN300).\n6. number_of_blades: null unit (12,14,14,16,18,20,22).\n7. main_shaft_diameter_inlet: \"mm\" (2,3,4,5,6,8,10).\n8. main_shaft_diameter_outlet: same.\n9. dynamic_load_rating_cr_inlet: \"N\" (286,644,1339,1646,2522,3369,6100).\n10. dynamic_load_rating_cr_outlet: \"N\" (286,644,644,1339,2522,3369,6100).\n11. static_load_rating_c0r_inlet: \"N\" (90,215,488,663,1057,1363,2600).\n12. static_load_rating_c0r_outlet: \"N\" (90,215,215,488,1057,1363,2600).\n13. qmax: \"m³/h\".\n14. qt: \"m³/h\".\n15. qmin_mr_1_20_pressure_0_100_bar_g: \"m³/h\", null where \"-\".\n16. qmin_mr_1_20_pressure_8_100_bar_g: \"m³/h\".\n17. qmin_mr_1_30_pressure_8_100_bar_g: \"m³/h\".\n18. qmin_mr_1_30_pressure_16_100_bar_g: \"m³/h\".\n19. verification_scale_interval (control element): \"m³\" (0.002 for G65, 0.02 for G100–G1600, 0.2 for G2000–G4000). Also drums counts... could add number_of_drums_before_comma, number_of_drums_behind_comma. Maybe include control element only; drums go in matrix table. I'll include verification_scale_interval as model_level and skip drums in model_level (they're in matrix_tables). Actually, let me include them too? That's 2 more × 19. It adds bulk. I'll include verification_scale_interval and skip drum counts in model_level (available in Table 5 matrix). Hmm, the schema example only populated qmax/qt/qmin in model_level. I'll include a reasonable subset: diameter, g_value, impeller_diameter, blade_height, vane_thickness, number_of_blades, qmax, qt, qmin×4, verification_scale_interval, and bearing ratings (4 entries: Cr in/out, C0r in/out) + main shaft (in/out). That's 17 entries × 19 values = 323 value objects. That's large but acceptable. Actually, to keep the response manageable, maybe I include main shaft + load ratings. It's fine — JSON output can be long.\n\nValues must be objects? \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape, separating the numeric value from the unit\". In model_level, the schema example shows values: [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}] — value is bare number there, with unit at the attribute level (unit_symbol on the attribute entry). Looking at the example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}. So model_level values are {model, value, footnote_markers?} with unit at attribute level. I'll follow that: attribute-level unit_symbol, per-value numbers/nulls.\n\nFor config_level: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value, unit_symbol}]}. Value structured? Rule 5 says values follow same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll do value as number and unit_symbol per value.\n\n**test_reports:**\n- {id: \"NMi-12200520-02\", date: \"22 September 2014\", pages: 39, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2514194-02\", date: \"5 January 2021\", pages: 10, role: \"test_report\"}\n\n**revision_history:**\n- {revision: \"Initial\", date: \"28 September 2021\", changes: \"Initial issue\"}\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"1 May 2025\", changes: \"Correction of the lubrication oil density in Table 1\"}\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06\"\n- revision isn't in schema... The schema certificate fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. The number is \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06 revision 1\". I'll put number: \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06\" and maybe revision... no field. Could append \" revision 1\"? Rule: don't fabricate fields; but revision history captures it. Hmm, I'll include number as \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06\" and the revision is in revision_history. Actually many extraction pipelines want the full number. The markdown: \"Number R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06 revision 1\". I'll set number: \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06\" — the \"revision 1\" is separate info. Fine.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3939314\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"1 May 2025\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijssweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nperson_title: not given → omit.\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Gas meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"TBQM\"]\n- module_designation: null (not mentioned)\n- description: \"Turbine gas meter\"\n\n**recommendation:**\n- id: \"R137\"\n- edition: 2012\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"1.0\"]\n\nHmm — R137-1:2012. Should id be \"R137\" with edition 2012? The \"-1\" is part number. Schema has no part field. I could note in... no field. I'll use id \"R137\", edition 2012. Or maybe I should reflect \"R137-1\" as id? Canonical examples: \"R60\". R137-1 is technically a distinct recommendation. Hmm. The certificate number itself is \"R137/2012-A-NL1-21.06\" using R137. I'll use id: \"R137\" and edition: 2012. Given ambiguity, maybe edition: 1? Let me decide firmly: OIML R 137-1:2012 — the \":2012\" is the edition year. In OIML-CS documents, certificates say \"Recommendation R 137-1:2012\" and the CS database records edition \"2012\". So edition: 2012. \n\n**Characteristics type_level:**\n\n```\n\"measured_quantity\": {\"value\": \"Gas volume\"}  → label: destined_for_measurement? I'll use \"measured_medium\" hmm. Canonical: \"destined_for_measurement_of\" → snake: \"measurement_application\"? I'll use \"measured_quantity\": {value: \"Gas volume\"}. Or \"destined_for_measurement\": \"Gas volume\". I'll go \"measured_quantity\".\n```\n\nLet me define type_level entries:\n- measured_quantity: \"Gas volume\"\n- environmental_class: \"M1\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1.0\"\n- maximum_pressure: \"See tables 2 and 4\" (string) — unit null. Hmm, include? It's a cross-reference not a value. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Including the reference is not fabrication, it's faithful. I'll include it as string value.\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-10, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- gas_temperature_range: {min:-10, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- humidity_rating / designed_for: \"Condensing humidity\" → label \"designed_for_condensing_humidity\"? I'll do \"condensation_protection\": {value: \"designed for condensing humidity\"} hmm. Keep label \"designed_for\": {value: \"Condensing humidity\"}.\n- orientation: \"All orientations (horizontal / vertical up / vertical down)\" — I'll use value \"Horizontal / vertical up / vertical down (all orientations)\". Simple: \"All orientations\".\n- lubrication_oil_type: \"Mineral base oil, type \\\"XZbase 100 A\\\"\"\n- oil_pour_point: -80, \"°C\"\n- oil_kinematic_viscosity_at_40c: 2.5, \"mm²/s\"\n- oil_kinematic_viscosity_at_minus_40c: 58.48, \"mm²/s\"\n- oil_density: 818, \"kg/m³\"\n- blade_angle: 45, \"°\" (constant across models)\n- flow_profile... installation stuff in config_level.\n\nunit_id: leave null everywhere (not certain of unitsdb IDs). Rule says \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\". I'll leave all null.\n\nFor units like \"mm²/s\", \"kg/m³\", \"bar(g)\", \"m³/h\", \"m³\", \"°C\", \"mm\", \"N\", \"°\", \"DN\" — use raw symbols.\n\n**config_level:**\n1. maximum_operating_pressure, axis \"bearing_lubrication_type\":\n   - {condition: \"permanently lubricated bearings, double shielded (DN 50–100)\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}\n   - {condition: \"external oil pump lubricated bearings, single or double shielded (DN 150–300)\", value: 100, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}\n2. prescribed_inlet_pipe_length, axis \"flow_disturbance\":\n   - {condition: \"severe flow disturbances\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"DN\"}\n   - {condition: \"mild flow disturbances\", value: null, unit_symbol: null}\n\n**Components:** The cert mentions \"measuring part, bearings\" as part of family characteristics but not as separately certified sub-assemblies with type designations. I'll skip components[] (rule: include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies — here none named with designations). Skip.\n\nNow let me build the models list with all data. Model id format: \"DN50-G65\". \n\nTable 2 data per model (DN, G → impeller, blade_h, vane_t, angle 45, blades):\n- DN50-G65: 51, 5, 15.5, 12\n- DN80-G100: 83, 12, 22, 14\n- DN80-G160: 83, 12, 22, 14\n- DN80-G250: 83, 12, 22, 14\n- DN100-G16